  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Nirmal Kumar Sharma vs The State Of Jharkhand on 21 May, 2014 Author: Aparesh Kumar Singh Bench: Aparesh Kumar Singh In The High Court Of Jharkhand At Ranchi A.B. A. No. 1651 of 2014 Nirmal Kumar Sharma .... Petitioner Versus The State of Jharkhand .... Opposite Party .... Coram: Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Aparesh Kumar Singh For the Petitioner : M/s Jitendra S. Singh For the Opposite Party : Mrs. Lilly Sahay, A.P.P. .... 02/21.05.2014",
    "facts": "The petitioner is apprehending his arrest in connection with C.G. Case No. 11 of 2014 pending in the Court of Addl. Chief Judicial Magistrate, Latehar allegedly for the offence registered under Sections 3(1)(zz)(iii)&(viii), 59(iii) read with Sections 26,27,48 of the Food Safety & Standard Act, 2006.",
    "arguments": "It is submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner that upon forensic examination by the State Food & Drug Laboratory, Ranchi on a sample of Laddu seized from the petitioner's shop, metanil yellow synthetic colour, which is not permissible synthetic colour, was detected for which he has been prosecuted. However, penal provision of Section 59(iii), does not apply in the instant case as no one has come forward to allege about any injury or grievous injury as a result of such consumption. Otherwise, even if the allegation is accepted, provision of Section 59(i) is applicable, which is bailable in nature as no injury is said to be caused to any person. Therefore, he may be enlarged on anticipatory bail as the alleged offence is not of such serious nature. Learned counsel for the State has opposed the prayer for anticipatory bail of the petitioner.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "It is submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner that upon forensic examination by the State Food & Drug Laboratory, Ranchi on a sample of Laddu seized from the petitioner's shop, metanil yellow synthetic colour, which is not permissible synthetic colour, was detected for which he has been prosecuted. However, penal provision of Section 59(iii), does not apply in the instant case as no one has come forward to allege about any injury or grievous injury as a result of such consumption. Otherwise, even if the allegation is accepted, provision of Section 59(i) is applicable, which is bailable in nature as no injury is said to be caused to any person. Therefore, he may be enlarged on anticipatory bail as the alleged offence is not of such serious nature.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Learned counsel for the State has opposed the prayer for anticipatory bail of the petitioner.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
